/// Chapter 15 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/10-components.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page ComponentsGuide(path: "/docs/guide/components", title: "Components", description: "Declare components: typed props, enum props, events, slots and scoped slots, parts, local state, stores, and how to design them.", layout: DocsShell("Components", chapter: t("nav.group.concepts"), blurb: "A component is a reusable element you declare: typed props, events, slots, parts and a body, checked like a built-in.", source: "md-docs/learn/10-components.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "a-first-component", label: "A first component"), Anchor(id: "props", label: "Props"), Anchor(id: "enum-props", label: "Enum props"), Anchor(id: "events", label: "Events"), Anchor(id: "slots", label: "Slots"), Anchor(id: "parts", label: "Parts"), Anchor(id: "local-state-and-actions", label: "Local state and actions"), Anchor(id: "stores-inside-a-component", label: "Stores inside a component"), Anchor(id: "what-a-call-puts-on-the-root-element", label: "What a call puts on the root element"), Anchor(id: "designing-components", label: "Designing components"), Anchor(id: "other-people-s-code", label: "Other people's code"), Anchor(id: "components-in-the-pre-rendered-page", label: "Components in the pre-rendered page"), Anchor(id: "quick-reference", label: "Quick reference")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/control-flow", prevLabel: t("ch.14"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/stores", nextLabel: t("ch.16"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("A *component* is a reusable element you declare yourself. It has typed props, the events it fires, the slots its caller fills, the parts it offers, and a body that draws it. The one thing to remember: the compiler checks a call to your component exactly as it checks a call to a built-in such as `Button`, so a missing prop or a misspelled flag is an error where you wrote it.\n\n**In this chapter**\n\n- Declaring a component and calling it\n- Props, including enum props\n- Events, slots, scoped slots and parts\n- A component's own state, and stores inside a component\n- What a call puts on the component's root element\n- How to design a component's interface")

        Heading("A first component", id: "a-first-component").h2

        Markdown("This page declares a `Greeting` component and places it once:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Greeting(_ name: String) {
    Text("Hello, {name}!")
}

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "A greeting.") {
    Heading("Welcome").h1
    Greeting("Ada")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The page shows the heading **Welcome** and the line **Hello, Ada!**.\n\n- `component Greeting(…)` declares the component. Its name is capitalised, as every element's is.\n- `_ name: String` is a prop. The `_` lets a call pass it without its name, so `Greeting(\"Ada\")` sets `name` to `\"Ada\"`.\n- The block is the body: what the component draws each time it is placed.\n- `Greeting(\"Ada\")` in the page is a call, written like any built-in.")

        Heading("Props", id: "props").h2

        Markdown("A *prop* is a value the caller hands to the component. This component takes four, with a type and, for three of them, a default:")

        CodeBlock(#"/// A person, at a glance.
component UserCard(_ name: String, role: String, active: Bool = true, avatar: String? = null) {
    Card.elevated {
        Row(align: .center, gap: .md) {
            if let src = avatar { Avatar(src: src, alt: name) } else { Avatar(initials: name.slice(0, 2)).primary }
            Stack {
                Text(name).bold
                Text(role).muted
            }
            if active { Badge("Active").success }
        }
    }
}

page Team(path: "/") {
    UserCard("Monzer", role: "Developer")
    UserCard("Sam", role: "Designer", active: false, avatar: "/sam.png")
    UserCard("Ann", role: "PM").active
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The rules for props:\n\n- `_ name: Type` marks the one prop a call may pass positionally. It must be the first prop. Every other prop is passed by name.\n- Every prop has a written type: `String`, `Number`, `Bool`, `Map`, `Any`, `[T]` (a list), `T?` (may be `null`), or a declared `type` or `enum`. See [Types](/docs/guide/types) for the whole type language.\n- `= expr` gives a prop a default, so a call may leave it out. A `T?` prop with no default defaults to `null`.\n- A `Bool` prop is set with a flag, `.active`, or by name, `active: false`.\n- A `///` comment above the component or above a prop is its documentation. The editor shows it on hover, and `wf docs` prints it.\n\nA prop that reads the caller's state stays live inside the component: a `Chip(pressed: isOn)` repaints when `isOn` changes. You do not need to pass a callback or re-render anything yourself.\n\nThe checker holds every call to the declaration. Leaving out a prop that has no default is `C01`; passing a prop the component does not declare is `C02`; a second positional argument is `E113`. A positional argument to a component whose first prop is not marked `_` is bound to that first prop, with a `C06` warning asking you to add the `_`.")

        Heading("Enum props", id: "enum-props").h2

        Markdown("An *enum prop* takes one case of a declared `enum`. The caller picks the case with a flag or by name:")

        CodeBlock(#"enum Tone { calm, loud }

component Note(_ text: String, tone: Tone = .calm) {
    Text(text) {
        style { padding: $sm; border-radius: $radius-sm }
    }
}

page Notes(path: "/") {
    Note("Quiet")
    Note("Loud!", tone: .loud)
    Note("Loud too").loud
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`.loud` and `tone: .loud` mean the same. A flag the enum does not have, such as `.huge`, is an error (`E103`) that lists the cases it does have.\n\nThe compiler writes a component's enum props to its root element as `data-<prop>=\"<case>\"` — here `data-tone=\"loud\"` — so a stylesheet can select on them: `.note[data-tone=\"loud\"] \{ font-weight: 700 \}`. See [Styling](/docs/guide/styling) for project stylesheets.")

        Heading("Events", id: "events").h2

        Markdown("An *event* is how a component tells its caller that something happened. The component declares it with `event name(param: Type)` and fires it with `emit name(value)`. The caller handles it with `on name(value) \{ \}`, as it handles a click:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }

component TodoRow(_ todo: Todo) {
    event toggle(id: String)
    event remove(id: String)
    Row(align: .center, gap: .sm) {
        Checkbox(checked: todo.done, label: todo.title) { on change { emit toggle(todo.id) } }
        IconButton(icon: "trash", label: "Remove {todo.title}").sm { on click { emit remove(todo.id) } }
    }
}

page Todos(path: "/") {
    state todos: [Todo] = [Todo(id: "1", title: "Milk"), Todo(id: "2", title: "Eggs")]
    action toggle(id: String) {
        todos = todos.map(todo => if todo.id == id { Todo(id: todo.id, title: todo.title, done: !todo.done) } else { todo })
    }
    for todo in todos by todo.id {
        TodoRow(todo) {
            on toggle(id) { toggle(id) }
            on remove(id) { todos = todos.filter(todo => todo.id != id) }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The checker holds each `emit` to the parameters its event declares (`T09`). A handler on a call must name a declared event or a DOM event; an `emit` outside a component is `E114`. A DOM event handler written on a component call, such as `on click`, attaches to the component's root element (see [What a call puts on the root element](#what-a-call-puts-on-the-root-element)). For events in general, see [Events](/docs/guide/events).")

        Heading("An event with several values", id: "an-event-with-several-values").h3

        Markdown("An event may send several values. The handler names them in the order the event declares them, and each name has the type declared for it. In this example `key` is a `String`, and `x` and `y` are `Number`s:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Entry(_ key: String) {
    event menu(key: String, x: Number, y: Number)
    Button(key) {
        on contextmenu(e) {
            e.preventDefault()
            emit menu(key, e.clientX, e.clientY)
        }
    }
}

page Files(path: "/") {
    state at = "nothing yet"
    Entry("notes.wf") { on menu(key, x, y) { at = "{key} at {x}, {y}" } }
    Text("Menu for {at}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A handler may name fewer values than the event sends: `on menu(key)` reads the key and ignores the rest. It may not name more. A name past the last value would always be `undefined`, so the checker reports it (`T22`). You handle an event that sends nothing with no names: `on close \{ \}`.")

        Heading("Slots", id: "slots").h2

        Markdown("A *slot* is a place in the component where the caller's content goes. The component places the caller's block with `children`, and may declare named slots that the caller fills with `name \{ … \}`:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Panel(_ title: String) {
    slot
    slot trailing
    Card {
        Row(justify: .between, align: .center) {
            Heading(title).h3
            trailing
        }
        children
    }
}

page Keys(path: "/") {
    Panel("API keys") {
        trailing { Badge("Beta").info }
        Text("Rotate every 90 days.")
        Button("Generate").primary
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `slot` declares the default slot, which the body places as `children`.\n- `slot trailing` declares a named slot, which the body places by its name.\n- Anything in the call's block that is not a named fill goes to the default slot. Here that is the `Text` and the `Button`.\n\nA fill is compiled in the caller's scope: it reads the caller's state and loop variables, and its handlers are the caller's. The component only decides where the fill lands. A page's `layout:` is a component with a default slot, called with the page as its block (see [Pages and routing](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing)).")

        Heading("Scoped slots", id: "scoped-slots").h3

        Markdown("A *scoped slot* hands values to its fill. The component declares what it hands over, places the slot with those values by name, and the caller's fill names them in order:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }

component TodoList(items: [Todo]) {
    slot row(item: Todo, index: Number)
    slot empty
    if items.length == 0 {
        empty
    } else {
        for it, i in items by it.id { row(item: it, index: i) }
    }
}

page Use(path: "/") {
    state todos: [Todo] = [Todo(id: "1", title: "Milk")]
    TodoList(items: todos) {
        row(todo, i) { Text("{i + 1}. {todo.title}") }
        empty { Text("Nothing to do.").muted }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The page shows **1. Milk**. The fill's names take their types from the slot declaration: `todo` is a `Todo` and `i` a `Number`. The fill is drawn again when a value it is handed changes.")

        Heading("Parts", id: "parts").h2

        Markdown("A *part* is a component of the component's own, called under its name — the way `Card.Header` is a part of `Card`:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Panel(_ title: String) {
    part Header(_ text: String) { Heading(text).h3 }
    part Footer { Divider  Text("End").muted.sm }
    Card { Text(title)  children }
    slot
}

page Parts(path: "/") {
    Panel("Keys") {
        Panel.Header("Rotate")
        Text("Body")
        Panel.Footer
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("You declare a part inside its component. A part has props, events and slots of its own, but it cannot declare parts: a part inside a part is an error. Calling a part outside its owner, such as `Panel.Header` with no `Panel` around it, is `C03`.")

        Heading("Local state and actions", id: "local-state-and-actions").h2

        Markdown("A component may have its own `state`, `derived` values, `effect`s, actions, timers and handles. Each placed instance gets its own copy, so two `Stepper`s count separately:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Stepper(_ start: Number, step: Number = 1) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    derived even = n % 2 == 0
    action move(delta: Number) { n = n + delta  emit change(n) }
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Button("−").sm { on click { move(-step) } }
        Text("{n}").bold
        Button("+").sm { on click { move(step) } }
        if even { Badge("even").info }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What an instance creates — effects, timers, listeners — is disposed of when the instance leaves the page. See [State and reactivity](/docs/guide/state) for `state`, `derived` and `effect`.")

        Heading("Stores inside a component", id: "stores-inside-a-component").h2

        Markdown("`use StoreName` brings a store into a component, as it does into a page. Every instance reads and writes the same store:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Cart {
    state items: [String] = []
    action add(name: String) { items.push(name) }
}

component AddToCart(_ name: String) {
    use Cart
    Button("Add {name}").primary { on click { Cart.add(name) } }
}

page Shop(path: "/") {
    use Cart
    AddToCart("Pen")
    Text("{Cart.items.length} in cart")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Clicking **Add Pen** changes the page's text from **0 in cart** to **1 in cart**. [Stores](/docs/guide/stores) covers stores in full.")

        Heading("What a call puts on the root element", id: "what-a-call-puts-on-the-root-element").h2

        Markdown("A component's *root element* is the one element its body draws at the top. Some of what you write on a call goes to that element rather than to a prop:\n\n- A DOM event handler: `Chip(\"x\") \{ on click \{ … \} \}` attaches to the root, so a styled button component is clickable wherever you place it.\n- `class:`, which is added to the root's own classes.\n- A `style \{ \}` block, which styles the root: `Tile(label: \"a\") \{ style \{ grid-column: 2 / span 2 \} \}` places that tile in its grid. Its rule's class joins the root's classes, as `class:` does.\n- An attribute the component takes no prop for: `aria-*`, `data-*`, `id`, `role`, `title` and `hidden`. You write it as you would on an element: `data-open: true` is `data-open=\"true\"`, and `false` writes no attribute, except for an ARIA state, which keeps `\"false\"`.\n- `mount:`, `update:` and `cleanup:` (see [JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop)).\n- `drag:`, the `drop…:` props, `on receive`, `on dragmove`, `on longpress` and `on swipe`. These need one root element: on a component whose body is an `if`, a `for` or several elements they are `C07`.\n\nWhen the component declares a prop of that name, it receives the value instead and decides where it goes. Declare the prop when the root is not the right element for it. This `Chip` takes the class and the description as props and puts them on its `Badge`:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Chip(_ label: String, extra: String = "", note: String = "") {
    Badge(label, class: extra, aria-description: note)
}

page Chips(path: "/", title: "Chips", description: "Styled by the caller.") {
    Heading("Chips").h1
    Chip("Beta", extra: "chip-beta", note: "Not finished yet") { on click { log("beta") } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The `on click` still goes to the root, which here is the `Badge`.")

        Heading("Designing components", id: "designing-components").h2

        Markdown("A component's interface is its props, events, slots and parts. These guidelines keep it clear to callers.")

        Heading("A value down, an event up", id: "a-value-down-an-event-up").h3

        Markdown("A component does not take `bind:`. To make a control of your own, take the value as a prop and fire an event when it should change. The caller decides what to do with the new value:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Stepper(_ value: Number, label: String, min: Number = 0) {
    event change(value: Number)
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Button("−", disabled: value <= min).sm { on click { emit change(value - 1) } }
        Text("{label}: {value}")
        Button("+").sm { on click { emit change(value + 1) } }
    }
}

page Booking(path: "/", title: "Booking", description: "How many seats.") {
    state seats = 1
    Heading("Book").h1
    Stepper(seats, label: "Seats", min: 1) { on change(v) { seats = v } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Clicking **+** sets `seats` to 2, and the component shows **Seats: 2** because its `value` prop reads `seats`.")

        Heading("Pick the right way in", id: "pick-the-right-way-in").h3

        Markdown("Choose the part of the interface by what the caller wants to do:")

        Table(caption: "Pick the right way in") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The caller wants to…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("configure it with a value")
                    Table.Cell("a prop")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("choose one of a few looks")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("an `enum` prop, set with a flag") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("put any content inside")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the default slot, `children`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("put content in a particular place")
                    Table.Cell("a named slot")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("render each item its own way")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a scoped slot, `slot row(item: T)`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("offer a sub-element with its own props")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a part, `Panel.Header`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("hear that something happened")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("an `event`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Keep state where it is read", id: "keep-state-where-it-is-read").h3

        Markdown("A component's own `state` is right for what only that component cares about, such as whether its menu is open or it is hovered. What the page or other components care about comes in as a prop and goes out as an event — or lives in a store.")

        Heading("Document it", id: "document-it").h3

        Markdown("A `///` comment above the component, a prop or an event is shown on hover in the editor and in the gallery `wf docs` writes.")

        Heading("Other people's code", id: "other-people-s-code").h2

        Markdown("To use a JavaScript library — a chart, a map, an editor — call it from a `.js` file under `src/` and hand it an element with `mount:` and `cleanup:`. To place somebody else's custom element, use `Element(\"tag-name\", …)`. You can also publish your components as custom elements for another framework to place. All three are in [JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop).")

        Heading("Components in the pre-rendered page", id: "components-in-the-pre-rendered-page").h2

        Markdown("A static build pre-renders each page to HTML — the *static paint*. It expands every component call into HTML at build time: props substituted, slots filled, and loops and branches over the seeded state drawn. A page built from components is therefore a complete page before any script runs. See [Static sites and SPAs](/docs/guide/static-and-spa).")

        Heading("Quick reference", id: "quick-reference").h2

        Table(caption: "Quick reference") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Syntax").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`component Name(_ a: T, b: T = v) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Declare a component; `_` marks the positional prop") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Name(x, b: y).flag`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Call it; a flag sets a `Bool` prop or picks an enum case") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`/// text`") }
                    Table.Cell("Documentation for the component, a prop or an event")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`event name(p: T)`") }
                    Table.Cell("Declare an event")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`emit name(v)`") }
                    Table.Cell("Fire it from the body")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on name(v) \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("Handle it on the call")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`slot` · `children`") }
                    Table.Cell("Declare and place the default slot")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`slot name` · `name`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Declare and place a named slot; the caller fills it with `name \{ … \}`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`slot row(item: T)` · `row(item: v)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A scoped slot; the caller fills it with `row(x) \{ … \}`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`part Name(…) \{ … \}` · `Owner.Name`") }
                    Table.Cell("Declare a part and call it")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`use Store`") }
                    Table.Cell("Read a store inside the component")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("[Components reference](/reference) lists every built-in, and [Diagnostics](/errors) every code named here.")
    }
}
